 # Standard Metric Computation
 
-Also called: NeRF Evaluation Metrics, Photorealistic Quality Assessment, Novel View Synthesis Metrics
 
 ## Overview
-Standard Metric Computation quantifies the fidelity of rendered images against ground truth references to evaluate the quality of novel-view synthesis in Neural Radiance Fields (NeRF) [S1, unofficial][S2][S4]. It addresses the problem of assessing rendering quality by balancing pixel-level precision, structural integrity, and perceptual similarity, providing a standardized benchmark for comparing different NeRF architectures and training procedures [S1, unofficial][S3]. What sets this family apart from raw error metrics is the inclusion of perceptual models (LPIPS) and structural models (SSIM) that correlate better with human judgment than simple pixel differences, while remaining computationally efficient enough for per-view evaluation during training loops [S2].
+Standard Metric Computation quantifies the fidelity of rendered outputs against reference data to evaluate the quality of novel-view synthesis and editing in Neural Radiance Fields (NeRF) [S1, unofficial][S2][S4]. It addresses the problem of assessing rendering quality by balancing pixel-level precision, structural integrity, and perceptual similarity, providing standardized benchmarks for comparing different NeRF architectures and training procedures [S1, unofficial][S3]. What sets this family apart from raw error metrics is the inclusion of perceptual models (LPIPS) and structural models (SSIM) that correlate better with human judgment, alongside specialized protocols for geometric and semantic evaluation [S2].
 
 ## Shared principles
-The core mechanism involves computing statistical or perceptual distances between a rendered image $R$ and a ground truth image $G$. All methods in this family operate on paired images, typically normalized to $[0, 1]$ in Channel-Height-Width (CHW) or Height-Width-Channel (HWC) format [S1, unofficial][S2].
+The core mechanism involves computing statistical or perceptual distances between a rendered output and a ground truth or reference target. Methods typically operate on normalized data, though the exact format (e.g., RGB tensors, point clouds, or depth maps) depends on the specific metric [S1, unofficial][S2].
 
 The fundamental metric is the Mean Squared Error (MSE), defined as:
 $$ \text{MSE} = \frac{1}{N} \sum_{i=1}^{N} (R_i - G_i)^2 $$
-where $N$ is the number of pixels and $R_i, G_i$ are the pixel values [S2].
+where $N$ is the number of elements and $R_i, G_i$ are the rendered and ground truth values [S2].
 
 From MSE, the Peak Signal-to-Noise Ratio (PSNR) is derived:
 $$ \text{PSNR} = -10 \cdot \frac{\log(\text{MSE})}{\log(10)} $$
-or equivalently:
-$$ \text{PSNR} = 10 \cdot \log_{10}\left(\frac{1}{\text{MSE}}\right) $$
 [ S2, unofficial][S1, unofficial].
 
