 ## Background
-Standard metric computation in NeRF evaluation originated with the seminal NeRF paper [Mildenhall et al., 2020], which established PSNR, SSIM, and LPIPS as the primary benchmarks for novel-view synthesis quality [S1, unofficial]. Early benchmarks focused on held-out frames from the training sequence (e.g., every 8th frame of a video), which critics argued did not reflect quality at extreme novel viewpoints [S3]. This limitation led to the development of protocols like Nerfbusters, which introduced separate training and evaluation video captures to assess performance on truly novel views [S3].
+Standard metric computation in NeRF evaluation has evolved to address specific capture and rendering challenges. Early benchmarks focused on held-out frames from the training sequence, which critics argued did not reflect quality at extreme novel viewpoints [S3]. This limitation led to the development of protocols like Nerfbusters, which introduced separate training and evaluation video captures to assess performance on truly novel views [S3].
 
-The inclusion of LPIPS, a learned perceptual metric, addressed the discrepancy between pixel-wise error and human perception, as PSNR often correlates poorly with subjective quality [S1, unofficial][S4]. More recently, metrics have expanded beyond photorealism to include semantic alignment (TIDS) and geometric consistency (Masked Evaluation, LiDAR filtering) to support text-guided editing and large-scale urban reconstruction [S1, unofficial][S2].
+Evaluation metrics have expanded beyond photorealism to include semantic alignment (Text-Image Direction Score) and geometric consistency (Masked Evaluation, LiDAR filtering) to support text-guided editing and large-scale urban reconstruction [S1, unofficial][S2]. Additionally, uncertainty-aware metrics like Negative Log Likelihood (NLL) assess the alignment between a model's output uncertainty and actual observations [S4]. More recently, bootstrapped approaches have been introduced to evaluate multiview consistency by retraining on rendered outputs [S5].
 
